 "Dc-5LEcE-O-": {
  "parts": [
   "This prompt is just invincible!\nPhoto source: Wind's Viewfinder",
   "Skill link: github.com/never…\nFull prompt ⤵️\n\nPlease turn each photo I upload into its own separate premium designed poster — no multi-image collages; output each photo individually. Use an overall 3:4 vertical composition, with the two areas, top and bottom, split strictly 1:1 in height, each taking 50% of the frame.\nThe top half keeps the original photo, preserving the subject's identity, structure, pose, real texture, natural light and shadow, and the original color atmosphere, with only a light premium color grade so that it has the feel of an art magazine, independent publication, and exhibition image. To fit the frame, the surrounding environment may be extended naturally, but the subject must not be stretched, distorted, or altered.\nFor the bottom half, first understand the **core theme, subject relationships, structural flow, mood, and visual metaphor** most worth remembering in the original photo, then reconstruct it as a **pastel crayon doodle illustration with a vintage paper texture**. Do not copy the photo object by object, and do not redraw everything in full; keep only the outline, pose, direction, and visual memory points that best represent the original, and through deletion, summarizing, slight exaggeration, and recombination, let people feel the correspondence with the photo above at a glance.\nDraw the subject with a **coarse-grained chalk / crayon-style hand-drawn outline**: lines slightly thick, loose, and dry, with powdery grain, intermittent drop-outs of color, slight tremor, and edges that don't fully close, and naturally blunt, rounded stroke ends. Inside the subject, add only a very small amount of pastel color blocks, simple grids, stripes, dots, or casual scribbles, using the least information to suggest structure, with no realistic volume or full detail.\nThe small surrounding elements use **the same line language** as the subject, but are further compressed into doodle symbols recognizable in one or a few strokes. Stars, flowers, plants, objects, environmental clues, or abstract symbols should all be simple, naive, open, and not fully closed, mainly single-line outlines with very little fill. Do not draw them as refined icons, stickers, or standalone small illustrations.\nKeep the composition in a relationship of **small-scale stamp-like marks and large areas of blank space**, arranging positions freely according to the subject's own direction, proportions, and visual center of gravity — it may be off-center, hug an edge, float, or be partially cropped. The subject and a very small number of doodle symbols form a loose but clear visual cluster, and the rest of the space is deliberately left empty. **The blank space itself is the main compositional element**, creating a sense of breathing, distance, and pause through the contrast of empty and solid, gathered and scattered, large and small, and asymmetry; better to draw too little than to fill it up.\nThe background must use an **extremely light, bright, clean paper color**, such as cream white, ivory, light beige-white, pale apricot-white, very light gray-white, or a near-white paper color smartly matched to the overall color temperature of the original image. The paper retains only very slight fibers and grain and must not look brownish, yellowish, grayish, or aged. **The background's brightness must be clearly higher than the subject's lines and color blocks, ensuring all crayon outlines, small symbols, and text are clearly visible and do not blur into the background.**"
  ]
 },
