 "Db_8Kt8E1uU": {
  "parts": [
   "DeepSeek is making up a belated lesson: Harness\nIn early July, Jensen Huang had a 26-minute conversation with LangChain founder Harrison Chase in which GPUs never came up once — he talked about a single word: Harness.\nHe said a company's future competitiveness lies not in model size but in the engineering system built around the model. LangChain ran an experiment with Nemotron 3 Ultra: without changing the model weights, just tuning the system prompt, tool descriptions, and middleware, the score climbed from its original level to 0.86, only 0.01 short of the top score of 0.87, while the cost per run was cut from US$43.48 to US$4.48. The model didn't change; what changed was everything around the model.\nThis is exactly the piece DeepSeek has been missing.\nIn early May, American independent developer Hunter Bown built a terminal coding agent, \"DeepSeek-TUI,\" on DeepSeek-V4, which shot to the top of GitHub Trending and passed 10,000 stars within a few days. This shows that overseas developers had long since taken the model to build their own ecosystem, while DeepSeek itself was a step slow at this layer. Developers could previously call DeepSeek's models and plug them into Claude Code and OpenCode, but the Harness layer that truly determines the experience was in someone else's hands.",
   "On 6/21, Harness team lead Cui Tianyi (崔添翼) posted a job listing to recruit\nOn 8/1 he publicly called for open-source projects to integrate — with no demo and no images, yet nearly a thousand projects poured in to sign up\nOn 8/5 he laid out clear directions: plugin, skill, MCP, orchestrator, aggregator, UI — covering almost the full stack of agent engineering\nOn 8/11–8/12, the DeepSeek Harness team's WeChat official account completed registration and verification, with a black whale as its logo, but it has not yet published anything; many media outlets generally read this as a sign the product is about to be released, rather than the product itself going live\nOn the model side, cost-effectiveness has long since been proven: V4-Flash's official release entered public beta on 7/31, and in the latest week (8/3–8/9) its call volume reached 8.83 trillion tokens, up 570% week over week, topping the global AI-model call-volume ranking. But V4-Flash's impressive results were achieved with the \"not-yet-released DeepSeek Harness\" at max reasoning effort, not the performance of the bare model.\nIn other words: DeepSeek has pushed model cost-effectiveness to the extreme, and the question it now has to answer is how to turn this capability into real, usable workflows in a stable, sustained, low-cost way.\nIn this battle, the target is very likely Claude Code and Codex."
  ]
 },
